#{[17592186342130 :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then   we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -12\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-5 \\\\ 0 &=-12.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0=-12$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution."] [17592186342132 :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}?$"] [17592186342132 :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-9 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting ${x}_{2}=-2,{x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+(-2)+3(-1) & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1}-5 & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=4$, ${x}_{2}=-2$, and ${x}_{3}=-1,$ which means \n\n$$\n4{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=4+(-2)-(-1)=3.\n$$"] [17592186342133 :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$"] [17592186342133 :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ -11{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-11{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-11{x}_{3} &=-18 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -15 & -30\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{15}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this value into  the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(2)=-6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, by substituting ${x}_{2}=-2$ and ${x}_{3}=2$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1}+(-2)+3(2) & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1}+4 & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=1$, ${x}_{2}=-2$, and ${x}_{3}=2,$ which means \n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1+(-2)+2=1.\n$$"] [17592186342134 :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$"] [17592186342134 :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 & -4\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0.2 & 0.8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0.2 & 0.8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{2}+\\,0.2{x}_{3} &=0.8 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+0.2(-1)=0.8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally,  substituting ${x}_{2}=1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-2$, ${x}_{2}=1$, and ${x}_{3}=-1,$ which means  \n\n$$\n-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-2+1-1=-2.\n$$"] [17592186342138 :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{b},$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$"] [17592186342138 :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=5.$ Substituting this value in the second and first equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{2}-2(5)=-6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=4 \\\\  & {x}_{1}+5=8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=3$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=5,$ which means \n\n$$\n3{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+4{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+5{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3+4+5=12.\n$$"] [17592186345006 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$  by inspection."] [17592186345006 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=2\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+0\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186345007 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ by inspection."] [17592186345007 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=2\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+0\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186345008 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$"] [17592186345008 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 16 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 16 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186345009 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$"] [17592186345009 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186345042 :question/worked-solution "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[B\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}I\\hspace{0.167em}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{2}:={\\color{blue}3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & {\\color{blue}3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186345206 :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186345206 :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}{x}_{1}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}+{\\color{rblack}{x}_{2}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=8 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 36\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{12}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=3,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}+3\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$"] [17592186345207 :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186345207 :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}{x}_{1}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}+{\\color{rblack}{x}_{2}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-6 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2}=-4,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-2)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}+(-4)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-2)\\cdot (-4)=8.$"] [17592186351408 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a diagonal matrix, we obtain \n\n$$\n|A|=(-1)\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1=-\\sqrt{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has two identical rows: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}-2} & {\\color{blue}2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}-2} & {\\color{blue}2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- Since $C$ is a triangular matrix, we obtain \n\n$$\n|C|=4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2=-8\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $|B|$ is equal to $0.$"] [17592186351409 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is a diagonal matrix, hence \n\n$$\n|A|=4\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 3=-24\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}0} & -2 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & 2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & 1 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ 1 & {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5}} & {\\color{blue}-2\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & {\\color{blue}-1} & {\\color{blue}2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|B|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"] [17592186360487 :question/problem "Consider the set $\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ of polynomials from $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=3-2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-6+4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)=0.$"] [17592186360487 :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ not both zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(3-2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(-6+4{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2})+(-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot column is the $1$st one. Hence, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the $1$st equation, we get\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{2}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2{x}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186360488 :question/problem "Consider the set $\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ of polynomials from $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1-t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1+2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2-3t-2{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}\\cdot {p}_{3}(t)=0.$"] [17592186360488 :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),$ and ${p}_{3}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1-t)+{x}_{2}(1+2{t}^{2})+{x}_{3}(2-3t-2{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})+(-{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3})t+(2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+({x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{3}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}=\\frac{{x}_{3}}{(-3{x}_{3})}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186360489 :question/problem "Consider the set of matrices $\\{A,B\\},$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B=O,$ where $O$ is the $2\\times 2$ zero-matrix."] [17592186360489 :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ not both zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B=O.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A$ and $B$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & -3{x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1} & {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} & {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & -2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} & -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. Therefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -8{x}_{2} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich has only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\nConsequently, $\\{A,B\\}$ is linearly independent."]}
